GELLER = custom({'g1':'K','g3':'Q','c1':'R','e3':'B','f3':'B','a3':'P','c3':'P','f2':'P','g2':'P',
                 'h8':'k','c4':'q','f8':'r','h6':'r','f4':'n','a7':'p','b6':'p','c5':'p','g7':'p'},turn='b')
GELLER = GELLER.rsplit(' ',1)[0] + ' 41'
GELLER_MOVES = 'Qe4 Qxf4 Qxf4 Bxf4 Rxf4 Re1 Ra4 Re8+ Kh7 Be4+ g6 g4 Rxa3'.split()

_add('exchange-combination', 'Use a tactical threat to reach a favorable ending', (368, 368),
     GELLER, GELLER_MOVES,
     ('Source Geller–Smyslov, Black to move on move41. Black has two rooks and a knight against rook and two bishops; both queens remain. The knight f4 is relatively pinned to rook h6 by bishop e3.',
      '...Qe4 moves the queen onto an attacked square and threatens Qh7, creating pressure on h1. Calculate the captures rather than judging the move by appearance.',
      'Qxf4 takes the knight and allows the intended queen exchange.', '...Qxf4 takes White’s queen.',
      'Bxf4 recaptures with the e3 bishop.', '...Rxf4 removes that bishop. Black now has two rooks against rook and bishop.',
      'Re1 activates White’s remaining rook.', '...Ra4 targets the a3 pawn.', 'Re8+ uses the open file for counterplay.',
      '...Kh7 answers the check.', 'Be4+ creates another checking tempo.', '...g6 interposes a pawn.',
      'g4 gains space and seeks counterplay.', '...Rxa3 wins a pawn. Black won later in the source game; the displayed position is not checkmate.'),
     (('A combination can aim at simplification', '''Many beginner tactics are taught as ways to win a queen or deliver mate immediately. The final example of this chapter has a different purpose: use a strong threat to force or encourage exchanges into an ending where the existing material advantage is easier to use. Black begins with two rooks and a knight against a rook and two bishops, an exchange advantage. White's bishop e3 attacks the knight f4, with the black rook h6 behind it along e3–f4–g5–h6. The knight is relatively pinned: moving it can expose the rook, but it is not pinned to the king.'''),
      ('Calculate the offer on e4', '''Black plays Qe4. The queen can apparently be taken by the bishop f3, but that capture permits Ne2+, attacking White's king while the knight also attacks queen g3. The source line continues Kf1 Nxg3+ Ke1 Rh1+ Kd2 Nxe4+, using checks to recover material. This is why an attacked queen is not automatically a blunder. The important conditions are the knight's checking move, its fork of the queen, and the rook's access to h1. Without those details, the same queen offer in another position could simply lose.'''),
      ('The game chooses a different exchange sequence', '''White instead plays Qxf4, capturing the knight. Black's queen recaptures, White's e3 bishop takes that queen, and Black's f8 rook takes the bishop. Count the survivors: Black has two rooks; White has a rook and the f3 bishop. The queens and knight are gone, and Black retains the exchange advantage. The game continues with rook activity against a3 while White uses checks to resist. The source reports a later Black win, but the displayed endpoint does not prove that White had no legal defence or that every exchange-up ending wins automatically.'''),
      ('Connect calculation with strategic purpose', '''Before calculating Qe4, Black knows what kind of position would be desirable: fewer tactical complications and active rooks against a rook and bishop. The combination supplies a route toward that aim. This joins the chapter's themes of planning, attack, defence and transition. At your rating, practise the method in smaller steps: name the desired endpoint, test the opponent's forcing captures, and count what survives. A combination is successful when its result serves the position, even if it ends in a quiet rook move rather than mate. Always evaluate the endpoint before committing to the first sacrifice.''')),
     'Play the source queen offer on e4, preparing the exchange combination and the threat Qh7.',
     (('Qe4',True,'The queen offer is backed by the checking knight fork after Bxe4.'),('Qd5',False,'This does not set up the demonstrated queen offer and tactical response.'),('a5',False,'This pawn move does not initiate the specified exchange combination.')),
     (('exchange-count','After Qe4 Qxf4 Qxf4 Bxf4 Rxf4, what remains apart from kings and pawns?',
       ('Black has two rooks; White has a rook and a bishop',True,'Black retains the exchange advantage after the queens and knight disappear.'),
       ('White has two bishops and both queens remain',False,'One bishop and both queens were captured in the sequence.')),
      ('exchange-fork','Why must Bxe4 be calculated carefully?',
       ('Ne2+ checks the king and attacks the queen on g3',True,'The fork supplies a forcing reply to the apparent queen win.'),
       ('Bishops may not capture queens',False,'The capture is legal; its tactical consequences are the issue.'))),
     ('Distinguish a relative pin to a rook from an absolute pin to the king.', 'A historical win is not an automatic result of every similar material balance.'),
     ('Choose a useful endpoint.', 'Calculate every forcing capture.', 'Count and activate the surviving pieces.'),
     example_title='Source fragment: Geller–Smyslov, exchanging into an active ending',
     extra_examples=(example('Source alternative: taking the offered queen permits a checking fork',after(('Qe4',),GELLER),('Bxe4','Ne2+','Kf1','Nxg3+','Ke1','Rh1+','Kd2','Nxe4+'),(
        'White considers taking the queen on e4 with the f3 bishop. Black has a forcing reply.',
        'Bxe4 accepts the queen offer.', '...Ne2+ checks the king and forks queen g3.', 'Kf1 answers the check.',
        '...Nxg3+ takes the queen with another check.', 'Ke1 moves the king.', '...Rh1+ keeps the initiative with the rook.',
        'Kd2 answers that check.', '...Nxe4+ takes the bishop and checks again. The queen offer has been backed by concrete recovery.',
     )),))
